\begin_layout Title
Null Hypothesis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To test empirical results against a rigorous baseline,
 a formal statistical Null Hypothesis (
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

) must be established.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In quantum foundations a Null Hypothesis must represent a world governed strictly by local realistic,
 classical probability laws without any unmeasured parameters or rotational mismatches.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because your framework tracks the data directly at the level of discrete,
 binary clicks (
\begin_inset Formula $\pm1$
\end_inset

),
 your Null Hypothesis can be built cleanly using strict,
 symmetric Kolmogorov expectation values.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The Local Realistic Null Hypothesis (
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For an ideal,
 noise-free local realistic system the expected joint correlation value 
\begin_inset Formula $E(AB)$
\end_inset

 between measurement 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and measurement 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is a function of their relative angle difference 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=\lvert a-b\rvert$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Under local realism,
 the standard linear boundary condition dictates that the expectation value drops linearly from perfect anti-correlation to zero following a "sawtooth" triangle wave function rather than a smooth quantum cosine wave.
\end_layout

